-- scripts/dst/latency_pair_dst.ail
--
-- WI-A14 piece 2, under ADR-001 D4:
--
--   "hold the request and underlying completion result constant while changing
--    only latency/clock advancement, and demonstrate the expected different
--    observed completion-versus-timeout behaviour. Both programs must replay
--    deterministically."
--
-- ============================================================================
-- S8 APPLIES IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, AND THIS FILE IS WHERE
-- ============================================================================
--
-- S8: when a guard asserts that X influences Y, check that X cannot reach Y
-- EXCEPT through the mechanism under test. This is literally an "X influences
-- Y" assertion — latency influences the observed outcome — and S8 was earned by
-- a defect of precisely this shape: a generator that reached its output through
-- a DECORATIVE path (the seed printed into prose) and passed a sensitivity axis
-- while every trajectory was identical.
--
--   * The decorative-path half. If the two worlds differed anywhere but
--     `duration_ms` — a timestamp in a payload, a duration in a message — the
--     pair would prove the ENCODING carries the number, not that time matters.
--     `scenario_the_pair_differs_only_in_latency` compares the two world inputs
--     FIELD BY FIELD and requires every field except `duration_ms` to be equal.
--
--   * The unwalked-branch half, which S8's complement calls the cheaper failure
--     to fall into. If neither trajectory ever ENTERS the timeout branch, the
--     pair certifies nothing and reads identically to a pair that does. So the
--     latencies are chosen to BIND — 40 ms under a 1000 ms deadline and 3000 ms
--     over it — and the branch is shown taken by reading BOTH OPERANDS of the
--     comparison off the recorded interaction, not by observing that the
--     outcomes differ.
--
--   * And the control that makes "except through the mechanism" executable.
--     `world_tool`'s guard is `inv.timeout_ms > 0 && t.duration_ms >
--     inv.timeout_ms`. The THIRD world is the slow one with no declared
--     deadline (`MOTOKO_TOOL_TIMEOUT_MS=0`), and it must COMPLETE. If a 3000 ms
--     tool faulted with no deadline in force, latency would be reaching the
--     outcome AROUND the deadline comparison, and the pair above would be
--     measuring duration rather than lateness. Nothing else in this file can
--     see that.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- THE PAIR IS BUILT ON THE TOOL CLASS, AND THAT IS A CORRECTION
-- ============================================================================
--
-- The handoff put D4's latency pair together with the `ScriptedStep` latency
-- widening cluster 10 deferred, on the reading that the pair needs it. It does
-- not, and the distinction is worth stating because it changes what is blocked
-- on what:
--
--   * D4's pair needs a class with a LATENCY CHANNEL, a DECLARED DEADLINE and a
--     COMPARISON between them. The tool class has had all three since WI-A12:
--     `ScriptedTool.duration_ms`, `ToolInvocation.timeout_ms` read through the
--     env class at `tool_phase.ail:343`, and `world_tool`'s guard. The pair is
--     buildable today and this file builds it.
--
--   * The PROVIDER class has none of the three. Widening `ScriptedStep` gives
--     it a latency channel, which closes cluster 10's declared D2 gap — the
--     generator chooses "a compatible response, fault, and latency" and chooses
--     only the response on that class. That is a real obligation and it is not
--     D4's: a provider latency with no provider deadline still has no
--     completion-versus-timeout behaviour to demonstrate.
--
-- Reported as a plan correction rather than silently taken.

module scripts/dst/latency_pair_dst

